{"type":"text","timestamp":1793587175485,"sessionID":"ses_e65e0cdf3ffe71S6IXyha5QUvA","part":{"id":"prt_19a203eed001ncviXFsGLTdKYq_text-0","sessionID":"ses_e65e0cdf3ffe71S6IXyha5QUvA","messageID":"msg_19a203eed001ncviXFsGLTdKYq","type":"text","text":"[goal] Decide whether to ship tutorial variant B over A from ./telemetry and record it in ./result.json.\n\n[action] Cleaned the export per README: normalized `variant` (trim + lowercase), deduped on `session_id` (70 resend rows, all byte-identical to their originals, zero conflicts), no missing/invalid `completed_tutorial` values. Ran a pooled two-proportion z-test (cross-checked with chi-square, sqrt(chi2)=z). Then stress-tested the clustering: 366 of ~763 players appear in both variants, so I also ran a within-player paired test on their per-player completion rates.\n\n[result] Ship B.\n- A: 342/900 = 38.0% completion; B: 420/900 = 46.7% (rounded to 0.4667); gain +8.7pp\n- Session-level: z = 3.72, two-sided p = 0.0002 (chi2 = 13.85, consistent), well under the 5% release-rule threshold\n- Robustness: paired within-player test agrees, B beats A by 9.0pp on the same players, p = 0.005, so the result is not an artifact of unbalanced player mix\n- ./result.json written and validated as parseable JSON with the required fields\n\n[next] None needed; assumption stated: the release rule's \"significant at 5%\" means a two-sided test (one-sided would be half the p-value, same decision), and identical ETL resends justify keeping one row per session_id. Analysis code is in analyze.py for reproduction.\n\nHow I know it is right: every number above was computed directly from telemetry/playtest_ab.csv by the quoted script runs (1870 raw rows -> 1800 unique sessions, 900/900 split, 342 and 420 completions), the two independent test methods agree on the p-value, and the paired check closes the main skeptical objection about repeated players.","time":{"start":1793587166969,"end":1793587175485}}}
